c trajectory datasets generated on the fly (e.g., compositions of oscillators at well-separated timescales, Lorenz systems, etc.), because no data can be downloaded and computing is CPU-only.\n\n- Embedding non-causal Hyena: In SiT block, the temporal attention layer (causal, over time dimension of trajectory tokens) is replaced by a Hyena layer with causal=False (non-causal). The Hyena operator: y = conv(filter_learned(positions), x * gating), with long convolution length matching sequence length, computed via FFT (subquadratic). Non-causality: kernel centered at current position, using full/symmetric convolution (e.g., FFT-based full convolution with centered kernel, causal=False flag in Hyena code), so each output token aggregates both past and future context. Also keep the adaLN modulation structure from DiT/SiT (modulate through time embedding) to preserve the SiT framing.\n\n  How non-causality is ensured: (a) use centered convolution (filter indexed from -L/2 to +L/2, not 0..L), (b) padding both sides equal, (c) FFT-based circular→linear conversion with symmetric padding, (d) in the Hyena code, set causal=False; the window mask is symmetric/bidirectional; optionally dropout masks (used for regularization) are applied in forward and backward directions (bidirectional masking). During training with stochastic interpolants, the time tokens are full sequences so bidirectional is fine.\n\n- Operationalize \"very long\": define a target horizon T (e.g., 4096 or 8192 time steps) vs. a \"short\" baseline (e.g., 256). \"Very long\" = length that exceeds the training length (length extrapolation) — i.e., generate trajectories longer than the training sequence length (e.g., trained on segments of 1024, generate 8192 or 16384), and/or a length at which a causal attention (setup_4) with quadratic cost becomes prohibitive or degrades. Also define via compute: attention is O(T^2), Hyena O(T log T). So \"very long\" can be operationalized as length where attention memory/time become the bottleneck. I'll propose: train at L_train (e.g., 1024) and evaluate generation at L_test ∈ {L_train, 4×, 8×, 16×} (e.g., 4096, 8192, 16384), with wall-clock and memory measured; \"very long\" threshold = at least 4× training length and ≥ 8192 steps.\n\n- \"Consistent\" trajectory: operationalize as (a) statistical consistency — generated distribution matches the target process distribution (e.g., power spectral density / autocorrelation matching, invariant statistics like mean/var per channel, Lyapunov spectrum for chaotic systems), (b) temporal consistency — no divergence/blow-up over long horizons: bounded amplitude, bounded energy, no NaN, and drift metrics (rolling mean/std staying within tolerance), (c) physical consistency — if a known ODE, generated trajectories remain near the attractor manifold (phase-space occupancy, e.g., correlation between generated and true invariant measure). Metrics: autocorrelation decay, PSD slope, spectral entropy, Wasserstein distance between marginal distributions of generated vs. true, and \"validity rate\" (fraction of long samples that stay bounded). For multiple timescales, measure with DFT-based spectral coherence: the model should reproduce spectral peaks at the ground-truth frequencies (peak detection / relative power at expected bands).\n\n- Metrics for multi-timescale dynamics: (a) PSD comparison (periodogram of generated vs. ground truth; relative error in spectral density, location and amplitude of dominant peaks), (b) autocorrelation function over long lags (ACF) matched to expected decay rates, (c) multifractal / detrended fluctuation analysis (DFA) exponent or Hurst exponent for long-range dependence, (d) wavelet spectral energy maps (scale-averaged wavelet power per octave) — this directly captures \"multiple timescales\", (e) multiscale entropy (MSE). Simpler: dominant-frequency detection via FFT and band power ratios; verify generated trajectories exhibit energy at all training timescales and no spurious dominating frequency.\n\nNow, \"time attention in SiT (identification of specific mechanism)\" — assumption: in the adapted SiT used for trajectories, the DiT-style block is applied to a sequence of state tokens; there is a temporal mixing attention along the sequence/time axis. In vanilla DiT/SiT there is only spatial self-attention over latent tokens; time is injected via adaLN from the time embedding. So \"time attention\" is NOT a standard DiT/SiT term — this is a key assumption/uncertainty. The researcher says setup_4 has \"original time attention\" and setup_5 replaces it. So the scenario assumes SiT was modified to include a temporal attention (maybe a causal self-attention over the T time tokens of a generated trajectory, separate from spatial attention). I should note this identification ambiguity as an uncertainty and propose a concrete operationalization: \"time attention\" = the causal/bidirectional self-attention layer that mixes tokens along the temporal axis of the trajectory (the layer we replace), while keeping spatial attention and adaLN as in DiT/SiT. Also note that the exact setup_4/setup_5 content (the NeurIPS paper setups) is not available in the workspace and must be treated as a stated premise from the researcher, not something I can verify.\n\nI need to be careful with the question budget of 6 — I have used 4 tool calls already (not questions to researcher; but the budget may count the researcher question turns). Actually the question_budget is probably for questions to the researcher. The researcher told me to produce plan and then they'll consider. I'll not ask many clarifying questions; I'll present assumptions explicitly.\n\nLet me also do a final check to confirm there is truly no dataset folder anywhere (e.g., /workspace was empty). Also check if there is maybe a hidden file. And check disk.\n\nLet me also look at environment CPU count = 224 cores, Python 3.14.4, no torch/numpy/scipy. So the design must either (a) assume we install packages (but network disabled, so cannot pip install torch — pip download impossible) — but note \"project dependencies not preinstalled\". Hmm. So actually running would require torch, but network disabled means pip install won't work. However, the task is design-only; the note says \"repositories and compute are not required.\" So we design the experiment regardless; we note that execution would require either a pre-provisioned environment with torch or a self-contained numpy-only implementation (pure NumPy SiT + Hyena via FFT is actually feasible — Hyena is convolution-based, and a reduced SiT can be implemented in numpy, though slow). I'll note this as an environmental assumption: if the design were to be executed, one would need torch/GPU, or a stripped NumPy version; compute limits (224 CPU, no GPU) strongly constrain model/data scale — must scale down (small model, short training, but we still define long generation via extrapolation and use small hidden dims).\n\nLet me verify numpy/scipy absence more concretely (already saw import fails). Good.\n\nLet me check disk space and memory to characterize compute limits.", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "free -h; echo '---'; df -h /workspace 2>/dev/null; echo '---'; ls -la /workspace; echo '---'; python3 -c \"import importlib.util as i; print('torch', bool(i.find_spec('torch'))); print('numpy', bool(i.find_spec('numpy'))); print('scipy', bool(i.find_spec('scipy'))); print('sklearn', bool(i.find_spec('sklearn')))\"", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-abaf414bb53e4bf7bc0f6021fcf0deea", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-abaf414bb53e4bf7bc0f6021fcf0deea", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "execute_check", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Check memory, disk and any hidden files in workspace\", \"command\": \"free -h; echo '---'; df -h /workspace 2>/dev/null; echo '---'; ls -la /workspace; 