{"id": "7884fc09-04c7-4201-991f-612a9d314ff0", "timestamp": "2026-10-22T14:53:06.382561", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "e865b0a5-caf8-4515-b591-ba018ba008e2", "thought": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "The approach is approved and the framework is locked. Now I'll develop the complete final design covering all seven requested points. I have already gathered the environment facts (empty workspace, no code/data, CPU-only, no network, no ML libs). Let me do one final quick confirmation of compute-relevant environment details before writing the full design.\n\n"}], "reasoning_content": "The researcher has approved the plan and now wants the full final design. I need to produce a complete experiment design covering the 7 points they listed. This is design-only; no execution, no invented results.\n\nLet me structure the final design carefully and completely. Let me think through each point in detail.\n\nThe overall hypothesis question: \"Does replacing the time attention mechanism with a non-causal Hyena operator in the SiT architecture allow the model to generate extremely long, consistent trajectories that capture dynamics over multiple timescales?\"\n\nsetup_4 = SiT with original time attention (baseline/control)\nsetup_5 = SiT with non-causal Hyena operator replacing time attention (treatment)\n\nThe experiment is a controlled ablation: only the time-mixing layer differs; everything else identical.\n\nLet me design this thoroughly.\n\n### 1) Rationale and pre-registered decision rule\n\nRationale:\n- Two model branches on identical synthetic trajectory data with known ground-truth multiscale structure.\n- setup_4 (causal time attention) has O(T²) cost and causal masking that may impede long-range consistency (error accumulation over long rollouts; autoregressive/causal structure may cause drift).\n- setup_5 (non-causal Hyena) has O(T log T) cost via FFT long convolution with a learned implicit filter (HiPPO-style smooth filter), enabling bidirectional context over long horizons at low cost → hypothesis: better bounded long rollouts, better spectral fidelity, comparable or better short-range fidelity.\n\nHypothesis H: setup_5 produces extremely long trajectories (≥4096 steps) with validity rate ≥ threshold, and captures multiple timescales (band-power fidelity) at least as well as setup_4, while scaling subquadratically (wall-clock/memory).\n\nActually the question asks \"does this replacement allow the model to generate extremely long, consistent trajectories that capture dynamics over multiple timescales\" — it's more of a capability question about setup_5. The experimental comparison gives setup_4 as control. The decision rule should say when the evidence supports \"yes\" vs \"no\" for the claim about setup_5 (relative to setup_4 control, plus absolute thresholds).\n\nPre-registered decision rule — the key thresholds. Let me define metrics:\n\nPrimary outcomes:\n- A) Long-rollout validity rate V(L_gen): fraction of ≥100 long samples per condition that pass boundedness (no NaN, amplitude within [−A_max, A_max] bounds, energy within factor), plus distributional consistency at long horizon. Thresholds defined from the ground-truth process statistics (e.g., 99% CI of the true process energy).\n- B) Spectral fidelity: band-power relative error per octave band and dominant-peak localization error. \n- C) Scale-up efficiency: wall-clock and peak memory per sample at L_gen.\n\nDecision rules (pre-registered):\n1. Capability-absolute: setup_5 must achieve V(L_gen=4096) ≥ 0.9 (90% of long rollouts valid), AND spectral band-power relative error ≤ 0.15 (15%) average across bands, AND peak localization error ≤ 5% of band center frequency at L_gen=4096. If setup_5 fails these absolute thresholds → \"does not allow\" generating very long consistent multiscale trajectories.\n2. Comparative (treatment vs control): setup_5 must be ≥ as good as setup_4 on short-range fidelity (sanity: within tolerance), and strictly better on at least one of: (i) mean validity rate ΔV ≥ 0.10 at L_gen=4096 (and ≥4096), (ii) wall-clock at L_gen=8192 lower by ≥ 2× (subquadratic), (iii) band-power error lower by ≥ 0.05. Combined with 95% CI (across seeds) not overlapping for the claimed wins.\n3. If setup_5 fails the sanity short-range check (degrades short-range fidelity beyond tolerance) → claim \"not interpretable / not supported\" because the replacement damaged the model overall (any long-range win would be confounded).\n\nActually let me structure the decision rule cleanly into tiers:\n- Tier 0 (validity of comparison): sanity control — short-range generation fidelity parity within tolerance (see controls). If not met, no claim either way.\n- Tier 1 (capability of setup_5): absolute thresholds on V(4096), band-power error, drift.\n- Tier 2 (relative advantage): setup_5 ≥ setup_4 on validity/spectral, and statistically significant (95% CI) better on at least one primary metric; if only parity on validity/spectral but big compute advantage → \"allows\" for compute reason; if setup_5 better on compute but worse on validity (statistically significant) → \"does not fully allow / mixed\".\n\nI'll make the thresholds concrete and fixed before \"run\". All tolerances registered upfront.\n\n### 2) Exact configuration of the two branches\n\nSince no code available, the architecture is my operationalization. Let me specify precisely.\n\nShared SiT (interpolant transformer) block — trajectory-adapted:\n- Input: trajectory as a sequence of T state vectors x_t ∈ R^d (d = number of physical channels, e.g., 5 for oscillator band). Actually tokens: sequence of state vectors indexed by time.\n- Patching/embedding: a linear patch embedding maps each state vector (d dims) into hidden D via learned linear projection plus positional embedding: E(pos) ∈ R^T×D using learned/sinusoidal positional embeddings (since time attention needs position info). Also a global \"time embedding\" for the interpolant time t ∈ [0,1] (the diffusion/interpolant time) as in DiT (sinusoidal + MLP), used for adaLN.\n- Block composition (SiT/DiT-style, same in both branches except the time-mixing layer):\n  1. adaLN modulation: from t embedding, produce (scale, shift, gate) per sublayer.\n  2. Time-mixing layer (THE VARIED LAYER):\n     - setup_4: Multi-head self-attention over the T tokens along the time axis, causal mask (autoregressive along time), D attention heads, head_dim, causal (masked) or actually for trajectory generation during training we have full sequence; in interpolation framework the vector field is learned from full sequences — so time attention could be bidirectional in training but causal at inference rollouts. Hmm. Actually for trajectory generation via SiT/flow-matching, the model is trained on full sequences (paired noise and data) and generates whole sequences at once by integrating the field; there is no autoregressive generation necessarily. The \"time attention\" here mixes tokens along the trajectory-time axis; in generation it processes the entire generated trajectory jointly. So it may be bidirectional (full self-attention) OR causal. The researcher's framing says setup_4 = \"исходный time attention\" and treatment is non-causal Hyena — implying setup_4 is causal (or at least that causality is a difference). I'll define setup_4 time attention as causal (masked) self-attention to make the non-causality the clean experimental contrast, and note this as an assumption/operationalization choice (the alternative — bidirectional attention — would weaken the contrast and is listed as uncertainty). Actually let me be careful: In the earlier plan I wrote \"causal (or bidirectional)\". For the final design, I'll fix setup_4 as causal masked time self-attention as the stated assumption, and note the uncertainty that if the original time attention was bidirectional, the contrast reduces to attention→Hyena rather than causal→non-causal. Then optional third branch (causal Hyena) isolates this.\n     - setup_5: non-causal Hyena layer (spec below).\n  3. Spatial attention: within-frame/state mixing across any \"spatial\" token dimension — for our 1D trajectory there is no spatial dim; we keep a lightweight per-token MLP (or drop spatial attention). To stay faithful to SiT (which has spatial attention over image tokens), in our 1D adaptation the \"spatial\" attention can be identity/MLP. I'll specify: a per-token MLP with GELU + adaLN (the standard DiT MLP) serving as the \"spatial/feature\" mixing, since a true spatial token dimension doesn't exist in 1-D trajectories. Note this as operationalization.\n  4. Residual connections around each sublayer; LayerNorm; adaLN gate (α(t) scale → residual weighting) as in DiT.\n- Depth: N = 6 blocks; hidden D = 128; heads = 4 (for setup_4). These sizes chosen for CPU feasibility.\n- The vector field / output: final layer norm + linear head producing velocity v_θ (x, t) ∈ R^T×d, predicting the time-derivative (interpolant/flow-matching objective) or the noise (if using stochastic interpolant parameterization). Use stochastic interpolants (SiT) objective: sample x_0 (data) and x_1 (noise), x_t = interpolant, predict velocity. Loss = MSE ||v_θ(x_t, t) − (x_1 − x_0)||² (flow matching).\n\nHyena layer (setup_5) — precise non-causality:\nHyena operator (simplest global Hyena, 2 branches / \"Hyena-Global\"):\n- Input x ∈ R^{T×D}. Linear projection splits into: x_v (value path), and gate paths via learned linear projections with GLU-style gating. For \"hyena\" minimal: v = Linear(x), g = Linear(x) with SiLU; filter h = MLP(θ(positions)) producing a filter of length T (implicitly parameterized), where θ is sinusoidal positional encoding over [0, T), MLP hidden ~D, output T×1 (per-channel filter or shared). Standard Hyena: h of shape (1, T) or per-channel (D, T).\n- Long convolution: y_filtered = FFT-based convolution of v with filter h, in NON-CAUSAL mode.\n  Non-causality implementation (concrete):\n  - Build a centered/symmetric filter of full length L_full = 2*T (or 3*T) indexed from −T to +T−1, with values symmetric h_c[k] = h[|k|]-style learned symmetric filter (or keep the learned asymmetric but evaluate with zero phase by FFT-shifting: use `fftshift` so that the filter's center is at index 0, i.e., kernel peak at position 0 rather than at position L−1).\n  - Convolution via FFT with \"full\" mode / zero-padding on both sides by (T−1) each, then center-crop the output to length T. Equivalently: compute circular convolution of length L_fft = T + 2*(T−1), with v zero-padded symmetrically; take the central portion. This yields each output token as a symmetric weighted sum of past and future context within the receptive field ~T.\n  - causal=False flag: in the Hyena formulation, no causality; also the gate masking used during training is bidirectional (a \"bidirectional\" mask variant as in the official Hyena code which has `causal` parameter; when False it uses centered filter and symmetric padding).\n  - To keep it exact and cheap: use torch.fft.rfft with L_fft = next fast length; complexity O(T log T).\n- Output: gating: out = v_filtered * g (elementwise with gate from g path), plus optionally a second convolution branch; sum → linear → residual.\n- This is a faithful non-causal Hyena: learned implicit filter (HiPPO-inspired exponentially-decaying or arbitrary via MLP), long convolution via FFT, gating (depthwise/element-wise).\n\nWhat is NOT changed between branches: input/output embedding (positional encoding, time embedding, adaLN), patch/embedding, per-token MLP (spatial/feature mixing), LayerNorms, residual structure, number of blocks/channels, optimiser, data, objective, sampling procedure. Only the time-mixing sublayer differs.\n\nWhat is assumption vs observed: entire architecture is an operationalization (assumption) since no source code exists in workspace; the only \"observed\" facts are environment ones.\n\n### 3) Data\n\nPrimary generator — multi-frequency oscillator bundle:\n- Channels: C = 5 channels to make multiple timescales representable. Each channel = sum of K=4 sinusoids with well-separated periods + small noise + optional slow trend.\n- Periods: to ensure ≥8× separation (spectral bands resolvable), choose periods P ∈ {8, 64, 512, 4096} in units of steps. Ratio 8× (64/8=8, 512/64=8, 4096/512=8). That satisfies \"minimum ~8× separation\". With finite L, spectral resolution: at L_train=512, the lowest resolvable frequency is 1/512 → the 4096-period component is sub-resolution in training segments; at evaluation L_gen=4096 it's resolvable (bin 1/4096). Good — this forces model to extrapolate the slowest timescale beyond training window → tests multiscale capture.\n- Amplitudes per channel, phases random per sample; noise: additive Gaussian σ_noise = 0.02 × (max amplitude) per sample; dt = 1.0 (arbitrary units; define timescale in steps, dimensionless).\n- Sampling protocol:\n  - Train: L_train = 512 steps per segment; number of train segments: 4096 segments for train, 512 for val, 512 for held-out test (generated fresh with different phases). Actually let me set train segments = 8192, val = 1024, test = 512. Each segment C×L_train. That's moderate size: 8192×512×5 ≈ 2.1e7 floats ≈ 84 MB — CPU-feasible.\n  - Long-rollout eval: generate M = 128 long samples per condition at L_gen ∈ {512, 1024, 2048, 4096, 8192} (the {1,2,4,8,16}×L_train sweep) → ≥100 long samples requirement satisfied (128). For 8192, 64 samples if budget tight (still ≥... hmm the requirement is ≥100 long samples; keep 128 across; maybe 64 for 16384). L_gen max 8192 (16×512).\n  \n- Lorenz-96 (second dataset):\n  - Equations: dx_i/dt = (x_{i+1} − x_{i−2}) x_{i−1} − x_i + F, i=1..K, K=20 nodes, forcing F=8 (chaotic). Multiple coupled timescales (fast waves + slow energy redistribution) for F=8.\n  - dt integration: RK4 with dt=0.05; subsample every 5 steps → effective step 0.25. Train segments L_train=512 (subsampled steps). \n  - Ground-truth invariant measure: attractor approximated by one very long reference run (e.g., 200k steps) from which we compute the empirical invariant distribution per node (histograms), Lyapunov dimension proxy via the correlation sum/GP correlation dimension d2, and mean/var per node. \"On-attractor\" consistency measured by comparing generated trajectory's per-node empirical distribution to reference (Wasserstein-1 distance), and checking the trajectory stays in the reference support (Mahalanobis-style / support coverage).\n  - Long rollouts L_gen ∈ {512, 1024, 2048, 4096} (Lorenz with 4096 subsampled steps is long enough given chaos decorrelation time ~ steps of order 10).\n\nVolume summary: train segments: oscillator 8192, Lorenz 6144; val/test smaller; long-eval rollouts 128 per (condition × L_gen).\n\n### 4) Controls\n\n- Seeds: ≥3 (use 5) distinct seeds, same across both branches (same data folds generated per seed? Better: same data for all seeds, seeds vary parameter init / optimization. Actually to test statistical reproducibility of optimization: fix data, vary init seed; also could fix init and vary data — choose fixed data, 3–5 init/optimizer seeds). Report mean ± std across seeds; also per-seed results (not just best seed).\n- ≥100 long samples per condition: 128 as above.\n- Sanity control (Tier 0): short-range fidelity — train loss curves (MSE), and short-generation metrics at L_gen = L_train=512: marginal mean/var per channel, ACF(τ=1..32), PSD band-power for the resolvable bands, validity 100%-expected (short samples trivially bounded). Set tolerance: mean ||μ_gen − μ_true||∞ ≤ 0.05·σ_true per channel, var within 10%, short validity ≥ 0.98. If setup_5 fails sanity parity with setup_4 (i.e., significant short-range degradation), comparison of long-range invalid → declare inconclusive.\n- Training-convergence control: both branches trained to comparable FLOPs/epochs and monitored train/val loss; if setup_5 underfits (val loss > 1.3× setup_4 best val) after equal budget, give setup_5 extra budget up to 2× (fix in protocol as \"convergence budget rule\") — or mark training-limited. Pre-register: compare at equal optimizer steps; if one branch hasn't converged (plateau not reached), training-limited and marked.\n- Optional (recommendation, not requirement): third branch setup_5c = causal Hyena (same Hyena but causal=True, filter causal, mask unidirectional) to isolate the effect of non-causality vs attention→Hyena replacement. If budget allows. This is explicitly optional.\n\n### 5) Metrics and analysis\n\n- Boundedness: NaN-fraction; per-rollout max |x| ≤ 3× (max over training data amplitude) per channel; energy E = Σ_t ||x_t||² per channel, requiring E ∈ [0.8, 1.2]×mean(E_true) (tolerance from true process ± sampling CI). Violation → invalid sample.\n- Distributional consistency: mean, variance per channel; ACF up to lag τ=256; PSD via Welch; compare generated vs reference with L1/L2 relative error and Wasserstein-1 on marginals (per channel).\n- Invariance: rolling mean/std at windows of length W (e.g., 256) along the rollouts; drift = slope of rolling mean vs t; require |slope| ≤ tol (e.g., slope of rolling mean stays within ±0.02·σ_true per 1024 steps). For Lorenz: Wasserstein-1 of per-node empirical distribution vs reference invariant measure, and coverage of the reference support.\n- Multiscale:\n  - PSD band-power: octave bands around each of the 4 known periods (bands: [P_i", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": {"command": "nproc; grep -c '^processor' /proc/cpuinfo; lscpu 2>/dev/null | grep -E 'CPU\\(s\\)|Model name|Thread|Core|Socket' | head -8; echo '---mem---'; free -g | head -2; echo '---disk---'; df -h /workspace 2>/dev/null | tail -1; echo '---python---'; python3 --version", "timeout_seconds": 60, "kind": "ExecuteCheckAction"}, "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a6759e817e794bea8fa54f82c5b86453", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-a6759e817e794bea8fa54f82c5b86453", "res