provisioning an environment with numpy/torch (not possible via pip here), or writing a pure-Python with math/struct? That's impractical. So the design should note: to execute, one needs a pre-provisioned environment; alternatively a compiled-numpy-based implementation. Since track.md says repositories and compute are not required, the design is the deliverable; feasibility section describes what execution would require and a realistic budget assuming numpy/torch available in a prepared environment (marked as assumption).\n\nLet me now write the result handoff. It's going to be long. Structure:\n\n1) Rationale + pre-registered decision rule (thresholds table with decision tree).\n2) Exact configuration of the two branches (setup_4, setup_5), non-causal implementation, block inventory (what doesn't change), assumption marking.\n3) Data generators with concrete parameters.\n4) Controls (seeds, samples, sanity, convergence, optional third branch).\n5) Metrics & analysis (all listed, aggregation across seeds).\n6) Feasibility in environment (compute budget, requirements, fallback scenarios).\n7) Assumptions/uncertainties table with assumption-vs-observed tags and effect on interpretation.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nKey thresholds to pre-register (point 1):\n\nPrimary metrics:\n- V(L): long-rollout validity rate at length L: fraction crossing all boundedness + distributional-consistency (+invariance) checks.\n- ΔB(L): mean octave band-power relative error (across the 4 bands, L1) between generated and reference PSD, at length L.\n- Peak localization error: |f̂_pred − f_true|/f_true per band, mean.\n- Drift slope: |slope| of rolling mean/rollout per 1024 steps, normalized by σ_true.\n- Wall-clock t_gen(L) and peak memory per full-length sample (or per rollout of length L).\n\nDecision thresholds (all pre-registered):\n- BoundAmax = 3 × (max|amp| over training set) per channel.\n- Energy tolerance: E within [0.7, 1.3] × mean E_true (L=512 reference).\n- Band-power relative error tolerance τ_B1 = 0.15 (15%) at L_gen=4096.\n- Peak localization tolerance τ_peak = 0.05 (5% of band center).\n- Validity threshold τ_V = 0.90 at L_gen ≥ 4096.\n- Drift tolerance τ_drift = 0.02·σ_true per 1024 steps.\n- Short-range (sanity) tolerances: mean|μ_gen−μ_true| ≤ 0.05·σ_true; var within 10%; ACF(τ≤32) relative error ≤ 0.10; short validity ≥ 0.98; val-loss ratio ≤ 1.3.\n- Compute advantage: t_gen(8192) setup_5 ≤ 0.5 × setup_4 AND memory ≤ 0.5× (subquadratic).\n\nDecision tree:\n- Tier 0 sanity: if setup_5 fails any sanity tolerance (significant short-range degradation vs setup_4 beyond the tolerance) → verdict INCONCLUSIVE (replacement damaged model; long-range results uninterpretable). If both pass sanity → proceed.\n- Tier 1 capability: setup_5 at L_gen=4096 must meet absolute thresholds (V≥0.90, ΔB≤0.15, τ_peak≤0.05, drift≤τ_drift). FAIL → \"replacement does NOT allow very long consistent multiscale trajectories (by our operationalization)\".\n- Tier 2 relative: if Tier 1 passes: support \"yes\" if (a) setup_5 ≥ setup_4 on V(4096) within CI (i.e., not significantly worse: 95% CI of ΔV excludes < −0.05) AND setup_5 not significantly worse (95%CI excludes worse by > tolerance) on ΔB; AND at least one positive: significant V-win (ΔV ≥ 0.10, CI excl 0), or ΔB improvement ≥ 0.05, or ≥2× compute+memory advantage at 8192. Mixed/partial: if setup_5 loses on V or ΔB significantly (95% CI) but wins compute → \"partially — allows longer/more efficient but with consistency loss\"; if setup_5 only parity on all metrics but strictly better compute scaling → \"allows (efficiency evidence)\".\n- Statistical criterion: mean over ≥3 seeds, report mean ± std; significance via 95% CI (across seeds and samples; bootstrap on samples within seed then across seeds). Only pre-registered, not p-hacked.\n\nAggregation over seeds: primary = mean over seeds; also report per-seed and worst seed (to detect that a single seed drives results). CI computed by bootstrap pooling samples across seeds; also report seed-level SD as reproducibility.\n\nNow data parameters.\n\nOscillator bundle:\n- C=5 channels; K=4 components; periods P = {8, 64, 512, 4096} steps → min ratio between adjacent periods = 8 (satisfies ≥8×). Amplitudes: base A = [1.0, 0.6, 0.35, 0.15] per channel scaled by per-channel factor (random lognormal ~ LN(0,0.3)); phases uniform; optional slow linear trend amplitude 0.1 per channel (drift stressor). Noise σ=0.02·(max amplitude). dt=1 step (dimensionless). Also add a mild random phase modulation per sample (new phase each segment) to prevent memorization.\n- Train: L_train=512; 8192 train segments, 1024 val, 512 test (fresh phases/amplitudes). Store on disk ~ (8192+1024+512)*512*5*4B ≈ 100 MB — fine for 20GB.\n- Long-rollout eval set: generate reference \"true\" long trajectories of length L_gen ∈ {512,1024,2048,4096,8192} sharing the same amplitude/phase seeds as the eval samples so that PSD/ACF/energy references are matched; M=128 per L_gen.\n- Spectral resolvability note: at L=512 only periods ≤ ~256 are resolvable (bin 512); the 4096 component is sub-resolution in training segments — by design, forces multiscale extrapolation; at L_gen=4096 its band (width ~ ±1 octave) is resolvable.\n\nLorenz-96:\n- K=20 nodes, F=8.0, RK4 dt_ode=0.05, subsample every 5 → effective Δ=0.25. Spin-up 5000 steps discarded. Train L_train=512, 6144 train segments (sliding with stride 128), 1024 val. Invariant reference: single long run 200k effective steps; compute per-node empirical marginal (histograms), mean/std, correlation dimension d2 via Grassberger–Procaccia on a subsample, and support (α-convex hull / bounding box per node at 99.9% quantile). Forcing F=8 ensures chaotic with multiple coupled timescales.\n- Long eval: L_gen ∈ {512,1024,2048,4096}, M=64–128.\n\nControls:\n- Seeds = 5 (fixed set 1000,1001,...1004); identical data across seeds; seeds control init & optimizer (and sampling where noted).\n- ≥100 long samples: M=128 at each (branch ×